  "order": 8,
  "title": "The add9 chord",
  "summary": "Add the 2nd, an octave up, for a modern, open sound.",
  "minutes": 5,
  "steps": [
    {
      "type": "explain",
      "title": "The 9th",
      "body": "Count past the octave and the 2nd comes round again as the **9th**: D is the 2nd above C, and the D an octave higher is the 9th. It is the same letter, just counted from the next octave.\n\n**Why it matters:** Chord names past 7 (9, 11, 13) count this way. Knowing the 9th is the 2nd tells you which key to add.",
      "exampleMidi": [
        60,
        62,
        74
      ],
      "labels": {
        "60": "C",
        "62": "D: 2nd",
        "74": "D: 9th"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "explain",
      "title": "Add9",
      "body": "Add the 9th to a triad, with no 7th, and you get an **add9** chord. Cadd9 is C E G D.\n\n**Why it matters:** It is the open, ringing sound of modern pop and worship music. Guitarists play Cadd9 more than plain C in many songs.",
      "exampleMidi": [
        60,
        64,
        67,
        74
      ],
      "labels": {
        "60": "C (root)",
        "64": "E (3rd)",
        "67": "G (5th)",
        "74": "D (9th)"
      }
    },
